# Caddy on ia-backend (<backend-host>). THE LIVE COPY IS /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
# and this file is not deployed by anything — deploy-prod ships the app, never
# the host's web server. Editing here changes nothing until someone copies it
# across and reloads (see the runbook at the bottom).
#
# Two jobs:
#   1. api.agenticcloudcomputer.com — the backend itself. Unchanged, and the
#      block stays FIRST so nothing below can shadow the control plane.
#   2. Any other hostname — a renamed site's OLD address. `agentbuilt rename`
#      repoints that record here, and the backend answers it with a 301 to the
#      site's new address (backend/src/services/site-redirects.ts).
#
# The catch-all is why on-demand TLS is on, and on-demand TLS is why `ask` is
# non-negotiable: without it, anyone who points a DNS record at this IP gets a
# certificate issued in our name, on request, for free. The ask endpoint says
# yes ONLY for a hostname some live deployment has been renamed away from.

{
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://localhost:3001/internal/site-tls-ask
	}
}

api.agenticcloudcomputer.com {
	reverse_proxy localhost:3001
}

# Everything else that resolves here, TLS issued on demand and only with the
# backend's blessing. A hostname that gets past the ask but is no longer a
# former name (renamed back while a certificate was live) reaches the backend
# and falls through to its ordinary 404 — never to another site's content.
https:// {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy localhost:3001
}

# ── Runbook ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Apply:
#   scp infra/caddy/Caddyfile root@<backend-host>:/tmp/Caddyfile.new
#   ssh root@<backend-host> 'caddy validate --config /tmp/Caddyfile.new \
#     --adapter caddyfile && cp /etc/caddy/Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.bak \
#     && mv /tmp/Caddyfile.new /etc/caddy/Caddyfile && systemctl reload caddy'
# Roll back:
#   ssh root@<backend-host> 'cp /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.bak /etc/caddy/Caddyfile \
#     && systemctl reload caddy'
# Validate BEFORE reloading, every time: this file fronts the whole control
# plane, and a Caddyfile that fails to parse takes the API down with it.
